<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Yuanzhi AI</title><link>https://martinsky999.github.io/en/</link><description>Recent content on Yuanzhi AI</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-US</language><lastBuildDate>Wed, 01 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://martinsky999.github.io/en/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Learning Guide</title><link>https://martinsky999.github.io/en/ai-foundations/guide/</link><pubDate>Thu, 01 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://martinsky999.github.io/en/ai-foundations/guide/</guid><description>&lt;p>This section is designed for developers with an engineering background. The goal is to systematically organize the core knowledge required in the AI era. Content is organized around two main threads: &lt;strong>theoretical foundations&lt;/strong> (Machine Learning → Deep Learning → LLM internals) and &lt;strong>engineering practice&lt;/strong> (AI-native infrastructure).&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="why-this-guide">Why This Guide?&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>AI knowledge is exploding, but most introductory materials fall into two extremes: either too academic (full of math proofs) or too shallow (just calling APIs). This section aims to take an &lt;strong>engineer&amp;rsquo;s perspective&lt;/strong> — selecting and organizing content by the standard of &amp;ldquo;can be deployed, can be reproduced.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Machine Learning Fundamentals</title><link>https://martinsky999.github.io/en/ai-foundations/machinelearning/</link><pubDate>Thu, 15 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://martinsky999.github.io/en/ai-foundations/machinelearning/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="what-is-machine-learning">What is Machine Learning?&lt;/h2>
&lt;p>Machine Learning (ML) is the core sub-field of AI. Its central idea is: &lt;strong>let computers automatically learn patterns from data, without humans explicitly programming every rule&lt;/strong>.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>Unlike traditional programming where &amp;ldquo;rules + data → answers&amp;rdquo;, machine learning works the other way: &amp;ldquo;data + answers → rules.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p>
&lt;h2 id="three-learning-paradigms">Three Learning Paradigms&lt;/h2>
&lt;h3 id="supervised-learning">Supervised Learning&lt;/h3>
&lt;p>Training data has labels; the model learns a mapping from inputs to outputs.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>&lt;strong>Typical tasks:&lt;/strong> Classification (spam detection), Regression (price prediction)&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Deep Learning Core</title><link>https://martinsky999.github.io/en/ai-foundations/deeplearning/</link><pubDate>Tue, 20 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://martinsky999.github.io/en/ai-foundations/deeplearning/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="from-neurons-to-neural-networks">From Neurons to Neural Networks&lt;/h2>
